{
  "result": "There is a quiet hour just before dawn when the world hasn't decided what it will be yet. The air is cool, still holding the memory of night, and the first light doesn't so much arrive as it seeps in, blue and hesitant. I love that hour not for its beauty, though it is beautiful, but for its permission. Nothing is expected of you at 5 a.m. The emails haven't started. The news hasn't refreshed. \n\nIn that hour, I like to make coffee badly, on purpose. Too much water, not enough grounds, drunk from a chipped mug that has survived three moves and one earthquake. It reminds me that not everything needs to be optimized. Some things can just be good enough and still be good. \n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be efficient, trying to turn ourselves into smooth machines. We track steps, sleep, productivity, even meditation with an app that congratulates us for breathing correctly. It's absurd and also human. We want to be seen, even by our phones. \n\nBut efficiency was never the point. The point was always the texture \u2014 the uneven stitching, the off-key singing in the kitchen, the book with notes in the margins. Perfection is sterile. It's the flaw that lets the light in, that tells you someone was here before you. \n\nSo maybe today, leave one thing unfinished. Let the bed stay unmade. Let the sentence run a little too long. Let yourself be a person, not a project.",
